e rule, we need to evaluate the constant expressions and replace them with literal values in the SQL SELECT list. In this case, the expression `2 * (SELECT COUNT(*) FROM t1 WHERE id = 1)` is a constant expression that can be evaluated to a specific value.\n\nThe transformed query would be:\n\n```sql\nSELECT \n    NULL AS col1,\n    2 * 1 AS col2\nFROM \n    t1;\n```\n\nIn this transformation, we have removed the CAST operation as it does not alter the data type of the column except to change or adjust nullability. The nullability characteristics can be preserved post-removal of the cast.\n\nThe rule AGGREGATE_REMOVE is not applicable in this case because there are no aggregate functions being used in the SELECT clause.\n\nThe rule JOIN_TO_CORRELATE is also not applicable as there is no join operation in the query.\n\nThe rule FILTER_CORRELATE is not applicable as there are no filter conditions (WHERE clause predicates) that may be applicable directly to either the inner correlated subquery or the main outer query.\n\nThe rule JOIN_SUB_QUERY_TO_CORRELATE is not applicable as there is no scalar sub-query, EXISTS sub-query, IN sub-query, SOME/ANY sub-query, or UNIQUE sub-query used in the join condition.\n\nThe rule PROJECT_REMOVE is applicable because the outer query merely selects the same columns as produced by the subquery without applying any transformations, functions, or renaming. Therefore, we can remove the outer query and use the subquery directly.\n\nThe transformed query would be:\n\n```sql\nSELECT \n    2 * 1 AS col2;\n```\n\nThis is because the original query was selecting a constant expression that can be evaluated to a specific value.', 'time': 4.0000529289245605}
